 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ for grid traversal, where $W$ and $H$ are grid dimensions. QEF solving is constant time per cell (2x2 matrix inversion). Binary search adds a logarithmic factor relative to resolution.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ for storing grid values, edges, and positions.
-- **Performance:** Efficient for moderate resolutions (e.g., 22x12 to 512x512). The iterative solver [S3, unofficial] is slower but simpler to implement; the direct QEF solver [S1, unofficial] is faster and more accurate.
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ for grid traversal, where $W$ and $H$ are grid dimensions [G]. QEF solving is constant time per cell (2×2 matrix inversion) [G]. Binary search adds a logarithmic factor relative to resolution [G].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ for storing grid values, edges, and positions [G].
+- **Performance:** Efficient for moderate resolutions (e.g., 22×12 to 512×512). The iterative solver [S3, unofficial] is slower but simpler to implement; the direct QEF solver [S1, unofficial] is faster and more accurate.
 - **Strengths:** Produces manifold meshes, preserves sharp features better than Marching Squares, handles arbitrary scalar fields.
 - **Limitations:** Requires robust normal estimation and intersection finding. Can produce non-manifold geometry if the QEF solution is degenerate or if the grid resolution is too low to capture complex topology. Clamping is not required as the implementation naturally handles boundary conditions [S3, unofficial].
